The established requirements are:

- Keep zero runtime dependencies and Node 22 or later.
- Preserve `driftship push <directory> --to <url>`.
- Default to four workers, 4 MiB chunks, and four retries, meaning four extra attempts after the initial attempt.
- User concurrency range is 1–16. Effective concurrency is `min(requested, capabilities.maximum_parallel_parts)`; requesting two must remain two. Use one global bounded part pool.
- Discover and validate server capabilities before begin. Malformed discovery or a protocol other than 3 must fail without guessed defaults.
- Resume defaults on through **`--resume=on|off`**. Reject `verify`. Remove `--no-resume` entirely; no alias is wanted because this branch is unreleased.
- Journal location: `<source>/.driftship/resume-v1.json`.
- Durable journal writes: serialize updates, write temporary file, fsync file, rename, fsync parent directory. No database or dependency to serialize writes. Directory mode 0700 and file mode 0600.
- `--resume=off` starts fresh locally. It must not delete or abort remote sessions or objects. All remote cleanup is out of scope.
- Local resume identity uses full SHA-256, size, and nanosecond mtime. Full-file rereads are acceptable.
- Validate `status.file_size`, `status.file_sha256`, and `status.part_size` before accepting resumed parts **or a completed session**. Mismatch is `session_mismatch`, exit 3, never silent session replacement.
- Missing or expired remote sessions are the only remote-state cases that restart automatically. A changed local file identity can start a new session.
- Only idempotent PUT parts retry. Retry eligible transport failures and HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504; respect valid Retry-After and abortable backoff. Authentication and checksum failures are permanent.
- Do not automatically retry begin or complete. After an ambiguous complete failure, one GET status is allowed. If that GET fails, return its error and let the next invocation resume.
- Exercise lost responses: a PUT may persist before the socket dies, and completion may commit before losing its response. No duplicate objects, skipped parts, or retransmission inflation of uploaded bytes.
- Reject destination query parameters, fragments, and URL userinfo. Only a trailing slash may be normalized. Preserve tenant path prefixes.
- Authentication comes only from `DRIFTSHIP_TOKEN`. Errors must not echo a supplied URL or token. Authenticated redirects must be rejected.
- Dry-run makes zero network calls and creates no `.driftship` directory. Scanning to calculate totals is fine; it may show requested concurrency instead of effective concurrency.
- Every JSON event uses `type` as discriminator and includes a single UUID `run_id` generated once per invocation. Do not rename `type` to `event`.
- Completion includes `files`, `bytes`, `uploaded_bytes`, and `resumed_bytes`. Uploaded bytes count confirmed unique bytes, not retransmission traffic.
- Scan skips `.git`, `.driftship`, symlinks, pipes, and devices; include ordinary dotfiles. Preserve literal filenames and Unicode without normalization.
- Exit codes: 0 success; 1 transport/server or ordinary local I/O, including permission failures and server checksum mismatch; 2 usage/configuration; 3 invalid or mismatched local resume state, including `session_mismatch`; 130 interruption.
- Cancellation stops scheduling new upload work, aborts requests, and settles journal writes already queued.
- Snapshots, locks, copy-on-write, and watchers are out of scope. Users must not modify the source directory during upload; detected changes fail. There remains an acknowledged race between final verification and completion.
- Do not claim production readiness. There has been no real storage-service smoke test. Such a test later needs a dedicated test tenant and credentials.

The protocol is documented in `docs/server-protocol.md`:

- Preserve the entire destination base path, including tenant prefix.
- GET capabilities includes protocol 3, minimum chunk 1 MiB, maximum chunk 16 MiB, preferred chunk 4 MiB, maximum parallel parts 4, and checksums containing `"sha256"`.
- POST `uploads` with `{path, size, chunk_bytes, sha256}` returns `upload_id`, `expires_at`, and `committed_parts`.
- Upload IDs are opaque, case-sensitive, and never used as local paths.
- PUT `uploads/{id}/parts/{part}` uses 1-based parts, Content-Length, and X-Part-SHA256. Only the last part may be short. Identical replay returns 204; checksum mismatch is 422/`checksum_mismatch`; different content for an existing part is 409/`part_conflict`.
- GET `uploads/{id}` returns state `open|complete|expired`, `part_size`, `file_size`, `file_sha256`, and unordered parts `{number, bytes, sha256}`. Complete status includes a receipt. Missing is 404.
- POST `uploads/{id}/complete` sends sorted parts and the full-file SHA-256. It atomically returns `{path, bytes, etag, sha256}`.
- Empty files still begin and complete, with zero parts and SHA-256 of empty content.
- JSON responses are consumed fully and capped at 1 MiB. Proxy idle timeout is 90 seconds.
- Object path segments are escaped; literal `%` becomes `%25`; Unicode is not normalized.

